        "role": "user",
        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Goals' (start)] The whiteboard is initially black, then loads a page with the title \"What are the goal(s) you can work towards this semester?\"\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 12 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The tutor's video feed disappears and a notification \"Diane has left\" appears on the screen.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The student's video feed disappears and a notification \"Kayla has left\" appears on the screen.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 8 seconds]\nTurn 1. STUDENT: All right. You can go ahead and make it big.\nTurn 2. STUDENT: And then you just hit exit, and then you go to this one. Bye. All right.\nTurn 3. STUDENT: All right.\nTurn 4. STUDENT: So you can make it big right there. Bye.\nTurn 5. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 24 seconds]\nTurn 5. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 18 seconds] The tutor joins the session. The screen layout changes to show the tutor's and student's video feeds next to the whiteboard.\nTurn 5. TUTOR: Hello?\nTurn 6. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Hi, Kayla. How's your weekend?\nTurn 8. STUDENT: What?\nTurn 9. TUTOR: How was your weekend?\nTurn 10. STUDENT: It was good.\nTurn 11. TUTOR: Nice.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p2 - '2143 ÷ 4' (start)] Tutor navigates to a new whiteboard. The problem is \"What is the remainder when you work out the answer to this division? 2143 ÷ 4\". Multiple choice options are A: 3, B: 1, C: 3/4, D: 0.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: Um, let's start with going over, um, the last question. Like, finding the remainder. Remember we were struggling on it? Let's see if you still remember.\nTurn 13. STUDENT: All right.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: Do you wanna write on the whiteboard or?\nTurn 15. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 7 seconds]\nTurn 15. STUDENT: I'll write on the whiteboard.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nPush for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
      }
